\title{Structural Register Allocation:\\Chordal Coloring, Degree Bounds, and Clique Spill Certificates}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{July 19, 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Register allocation assigns simultaneously live program variables to a finite bank of processor registers. Modeling variables as vertices and simultaneous liveness as adjacency turns allocation into graph coloring, while spilling becomes vertex deletion before coloring. This paper separates maximum degree, clique number, and chromatic number. For every finite interference graph, a palette strictly larger than the maximum degree is sufficient, but this bound need not be exact: the three-vertex path requires two colors although its maximum degree plus one is three. Exactness is recovered under an explicit structural hypothesis. If the graph admits a perfect elimination ordering, then it is colorable with $k$ registers if and only if every clique has size at most $k$; hence its chromatic number equals its clique number. With $k$ registers, a clique of size $m>k$ also forces at least $m-k$ spills from that clique. These results support elimination-based allocation and clique-aware spill analysis while ruling out universal claims that degree determines exact demand or optimal spills.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
A register is a scarce, fast storage location. Every temporary value has a live range, the portion of execution during which its current value may still be needed. Two values whose live ranges overlap cannot safely occupy the same register. An allocator must exploit non-overlap to reuse registers; if too few registers are available, selected values are \emph{spilled} to memory.

The standard model is an interference graph: vertices are variables and edges record simultaneous liveness. Registers become colors and valid allocation becomes proper vertex coloring. This translation suggests three statistics. Maximum degree measures worst local congestion, clique number measures the largest pairwise-conflicting family, and chromatic number measures exact register demand. Although related, these quantities are not interchangeable.

The universal greedy bound gives $\chi(G)\leq\Delta(G)+1$, but it gives no equality. The path on three vertices already separates the two sides: its middle vertex has two neighbors, but its endpoints can reuse a color. The constructive positive result instead comes from chordal structure. In a perfect elimination ordering, each relevant later neighborhood is a clique. Reverse greedy coloring then turns clique bounds into an exact allocation algorithm.

This paper develops four conclusions: a palette characterization for perfect elimination graphs; a universal degree-based sufficiency theorem; a minimal counterexample to degree exactness; and a clique lower bound on forced spills.

\section{Model and definitions}
\begin{definition}[Interference graph]
A finite interference graph is a simple graph $G=(V,E)$. Its vertices represent program values, and $\{u,v\}\in E$ means that $u$ and $v$ are simultaneously live and cannot share a register.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Coloring and chromatic number]
A proper $k$-coloring is a map $c:V\to\{1,\ldots,k\}$ satisfying $c(u)\neq c(v)$ for every edge $\{u,v\}$. The least feasible $k$ is the chromatic number $\chi(G)$.
\end{definition}

For $v\in V$, let $N(v)=\{u:\{u,v\}\in E\}$ and $\deg(v)=|N(v)|$. The maximum degree is $\Delta(G)=\max_v\deg(v)$. A clique is a subset whose distinct vertices are pairwise adjacent; the largest clique size is $\omega(G)$. Since all members of a clique need distinct colors,
\[
\omega(G)\leq\chi(G).
\]

\begin{definition}[Perfect elimination ordering]
An ordering $v_1,\ldots,v_n$ is perfect if, for every $i$, the later neighbors
\[
N_i^+(v_i)=\{v_j:j>i\text{ and }\{v_i,v_j\}\in E\}
\]
form a clique.
\end{definition}

A graph is chordal when every cycle of length at least four has an edge joining two nonconsecutive cycle vertices. Finite chordal graphs are exactly those admitting perfect elimination orderings. The ordering, rather than the name of the class, is the operative allocation certificate.

\begin{definition}[Spill set]
For a register budget $k$, a spill set is $R\subseteq V$ such that the induced graph on $V\setminus R$ is $k$-colorable. Unit-cost spilling minimizes $|R|$; weighted spilling assigns a nonnegative cost $w(v)$ and minimizes $\sum_{v\in R}w(v)$.
\end{definition}

\section{Universal degree control}
\begin{theorem}[Degree-budget sufficiency]
If $G$ is finite and $\Delta(G)<k$, then $G$ is $k$-colorable. Equivalently,
\[
\chi(G)\leq\Delta(G)+1.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Order the vertices arbitrarily and color them one at a time. At vertex $v$, at most $\deg(v)\leq\Delta(G)$ colors are forbidden by colored neighbors. Since $k>\Delta(G)$, one color remains. Continuing gives a proper coloring.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Failure criterion for the degree formula]
If $G$ is $k$-colorable and $k<\Delta(G)+1$, then $\chi(G)\neq\Delta(G)+1$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
A $k$-coloring gives $\chi(G)\leq k<\Delta(G)+1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Three-vertex separation]
For the path $P_3$ with edges $\{a,b\}$ and $\{b,c\}$,
\[
\chi(P_3)=2<3=\Delta(P_3)+1.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
One color is impossible because the graph has an edge. Color $a,c$ alike and $b$ differently, proving that two colors suffice. The middle vertex has degree two, while the endpoints have degree one.
\end{proof}

This example also refutes the assertion that a budget below $\Delta(G)+1$ necessarily causes spilling: $P_3$ uses two registers without spills. Degree counts both conflicts incident to $b$, but the nonadjacent endpoints may reuse one register.

\section{Exact allocation from elimination structure}
\begin{theorem}[Chordal Register Palette Theorem]
Let $G$ be a finite graph with a perfect elimination ordering. For every integer $k\geq0$, the following are equivalent:
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*)]
\item $G$ has a proper $k$-coloring;
\item every clique $S$ of $G$ has $|S|\leq k$.
\end{enumerate}
Consequently, $\chi(G)=\omega(G)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
If a $k$-coloring exists, members of a clique have pairwise distinct colors, so no clique has more than $k$ vertices.

Conversely, let $v_1,\ldots,v_n$ be a perfect elimination ordering and color in reverse order. When $v_i$ is reached, its already colored neighbors are exactly among $N_i^+(v_i)$, which is a clique. Moreover, $N_i^+(v_i)\cup\{v_i\}$ is a clique and therefore has at most $k$ vertices. Thus at most $k-1$ colors are forbidden and an available color exists. Induction completes the coloring. The general lower bound $\omega(G)\leq\chi(G)$ and the constructed $\omega(G)$-coloring give equality.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[No-spill criterion]
For a graph with a perfect elimination ordering, $k$ registers permit spill-free allocation if and only if no clique has more than $k$ vertices.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[Interval-liveness exactness]
If live ranges are intervals on a line and interference means interval overlap, then $\chi(G)=\omega(G)$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Repeatedly choose an interval whose right endpoint is earliest. Every remaining interval intersecting it contains that endpoint, so those neighbors pairwise intersect. This produces a perfect elimination ordering; the preceding theorem applies.
\end{proof}

The semantic hypothesis is essential. General control-flow liveness need not consist of intervals on one line, and a syntactic description of a program does not by itself establish chordality. The liveness representation must justify the elimination structure.

\section{Clique pressure and spills}
\begin{theorem}[Clique Spill Bound]
Let $S$ be a clique of size $m$ in a finite interference graph, and let $k<m$. For every spill set $R$ whose unspilled graph is $k$-colorable,
\[
|S\cap R|\geq m-k.
\]
Thus at least $m-k$ members of $S$ must spill.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The set $S\setminus R$ remains a clique in the unspilled graph, so its members require distinct colors and $|S\setminus R|\leq k$. Since
\[
m=|S|=|S\setminus R|+|S\cap R|,
\]
we have $|S\cap R|=m-|S\setminus R|\geq m-k$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
Every valid spill set in the preceding theorem satisfies $|R|\geq m-k$.
\end{corollary}

For weighted spilling, the theorem remains a cardinality constraint but does not determine minimum cost. Within one overloaded clique, a cost lower bound is the sum of the $m-k$ smallest vertex costs. Globally, maximal cliques overlap, and one spilled vertex can relieve several constraints. Degree records neither this overlap nor execution-frequency costs, so maximum-degree deletion has no universal optimality guarantee.

\section{Algorithms}
\subsection{Elimination-order coloring}
Given $v_1,\ldots,v_n$, process vertices from $v_n$ to $v_1$. At each vertex, collect colors used by already colored neighbors and choose an unused color. Under the clique bound, the palette theorem guarantees success. With adjacency lists, the coloring phase takes $O(|V|+|E|)$ time after the ordering is supplied, since every edge is inspected only constantly many times. Chordality recognition and ordering construction can likewise be performed in linear time.

\subsection{Exact coloring for small instances}
For an arbitrary small graph, test $k=1,2,\ldots,|V|$. For each $k$, recursively select an uncolored vertex and try colors absent from its colored neighborhood. The first feasible $k$ is $\chi(G)$. Selecting a high-degree uncolored vertex often prunes the search, although worst-case complexity remains exponential, as expected for general coloring.

\subsection{Clique certificates}
Given a proposed clique $S$, verify all vertex pairs in $O(|S|^2)$ adjacency queries. Then report $\max(0,|S|-k)$ as a forced-spill lower bound. Several overlapping certificates remain individually valid, but they cannot simply be summed because one spill may satisfy more than one certificate.

\section{Examples and boundaries}
Every nontrivial tree is bipartite and has no clique larger than two, so $\chi(T)=\omega(T)=2$. A star with $r$ leaves has $\Delta=r$ but still needs only two colors. Hence the gap between $\Delta+1$ and $\chi$ can be arbitrarily large even among chordal graphs.

For a complete graph $K_n$, every pair interferes and
\[
\chi(K_n)=\omega(K_n)=n=\Delta(K_n)+1.
\]
Here local and collective congestion coincide, so the degree bound is exact.

For an odd cycle $C_{2r+1}$ with $r\geq2$,
\[
\omega(C_{2r+1})=2<3=\chi(C_{2r+1}).
\]
Such a cycle is not chordal. It shows that clique number alone cannot control coloring outside a suitable structural class.

As a spill example, seven pairwise interfering variables with four registers force at least $7-4=3$ spills from that clique. A second clique of size six independently forces two spills among its members, but overlapping clique bounds cannot be added without accounting for shared vertices.

\section{Applications and discussion}
A disciplined allocation pipeline begins with explicit liveness semantics and its interference graph. It next searches for a perfect elimination ordering. If one exists, maximum clique size gives exact register demand and reverse elimination supplies the assignment. If the budget is smaller, overloaded cliques certify unavoidable spills. If no elimination structure exists, the degree theorem remains a safe upper bound, but odd-cycle obstructions show that clique size may underestimate demand.

The framework applies to any finite resource problem with pairwise incompatibility, including interval scheduling, frequency assignment, temporary storage planning, and classroom allocation. Interval or chordal structure changes a difficult global coloring task into a greedy algorithm with exact obstruction certificates.

The key conceptual distinction is between local conflict count and collective conflict structure. Maximum degree bounds how many colors a greedy step might avoid in the worst case. Clique number measures colors simultaneously forced. A perfect elimination ordering bridges them by ensuring that each relevant already colored neighborhood is a clique. The path counterexample and the palette theorem are two sides of this observation.

Spilling adds a separate optimization problem. Clique pressure supplies necessary local deletions, but costs and overlapping cliques determine which deletion is economical. A static degree ranking omits both. A principled optimizer should use maximal-clique arrangement, variable costs, and the changing effect of each spill.

\section{Future directions}
Weighted spilling on chordal graphs invites dynamic programming over clique trees. Chordality localizes obstructions to maximal cliques, while a clique tree records their overlaps; state spaces parameterized by clique size may yield exact tractable algorithms.

Modern processors also divide registers into classes. This motivates list coloring, in which each variable has an admissible register set. A local condition based on the largest clique containing each vertex may strengthen the uniform palette theorem.

A semantic characterization of chordal interference is equally important. Live ranges represented by connected subtrees of a dominance tree should produce chordal intersection graphs, and a converse representation would tightly connect control-flow semantics to elimination structure.

Restricted graph classes may support valid maximum-degree spilling rules. Block graphs are a natural candidate for identifying the exact boundary at which local degree and clique congestion align. Finally, graphs made chordal by deleting a small exceptional set suggest fixed-parameter algorithms in the exceptional-set size and register budget.

\section{Conclusion}
Every finite interference graph is colorable with $\Delta(G)+1$ registers, but this is a safe ceiling rather than an exact forecast. The three-vertex path needs only two registers despite having maximum degree two. Under a perfect elimination ordering, the exact criterion is clique-based: $k$ registers suffice precisely when every clique has size at most $k$, so $\chi(G)=\omega(G)$. When a clique of size $m$ faces only $k<m$ registers, at least $m-k$ of its members must spill.

These statements provide three distinct tools: a universal budget, an exact structural allocation theorem, and a local certificate of unavoidable spilling. Keeping their roles separate prevents upper bounds from becoming false equalities and turns elimination structure into an effective guide for resource assignment.
\end{document}

